This paper investigates the normalized ground state solution for the given biharmonic Choquard type equation \begin{align*}
  \begin{split}
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
  \Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda u+\left(\frac{1}{|x|^\mu}*F(u)\right)f(u),
  \quad\mbox{in}\ \ \mathbb{R}^4,
  \displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^4}|u|^2dx=c^2,\quad u\in H^2(\mathbb{R}^4),
  \end{array}
  \right.
  \end{split}
  \end{align*} under specific constraints and growth conditions for $\beta$, $\lambda$, and $f$, and we leverage a homotopy-based minimax principle to establish the existence of a normalized ground state solution for this equation. Additionally, we investigate the multiplicity of the solution by employing a local argument and variational mountain pass charaterization. Furthermore, we obtain a result regarding the multiplicity of the solution depending on the parameter $\beta$. Finally, we present an application involving an integral equation to demonstrate the flexibility of our approach. To the best of our knowledge, this work contributes notably to the study of the biharmonic Choquard type equation with critical growth. 

  Specifically, this work goes beyond previous research in several key aspects. Firstly, previous research concerning the Choquard type equation has primarily focused on the Schrödinger equation. Secondly, previous studies have typically addressed the equation subject to Dirichlet boundary conditions on a bounded domain, whereas this work investigates the equation in the whole space. Thirdly, previous studies have typically analyzed the equation with subcritical growth in the boundary data, whereas this work investigates the equation with critical growth in the boundary data. Fourthly, previous research has primarily established the existence of a ground state solution, whereas this work establishes both the existence and the multiplicity of a ground state solution. Lastly, this work is an extension of a previous paper by J. Liu et al. titled "Normalized ground states for a biharmonic equation with singular nonlinearities and boundary conditions" presented at the 2020 ISMP conference in Madrid, Spain. This work is an expansion of the previous conference paper in which the singular nonlinearities were replaced with a non-singular nonlinearity with a critical growth. This work

